---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Plan a trip"
  description="How to get to Tioman and to Kampung Mukut, when to go, the trail checkpoint system, and what to expect on the Dragon's Horns."
  section="/plan"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/#areas">Guidebook</a><span>/</span><span>Plan a trip</span>
    </nav>

    <section>
      <div class="sec-head"><h2>Plan a trip</h2></div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:1.25rem">
          <div class="panel">
            <span class="label">Getting there</span>
            <h3>There is no road to Mukut</h3>
            <p>
              Ferry from <strong>Mersing</strong> or <strong>Tanjung Gemok</strong> to Tioman's west
              coast, then a sea taxi round the south coast to Mukut — or the coastal trail on foot.
              Some parties have kayaked from Juara.
            </p>
            <p>
              Mumbar, Bagus and Batu Sirau are reached from the <strong>Nipah</strong> side, along
              the Nipah–Tunamaya concrete path and then jungle trails.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <span class="label">Approaches</span>
            <h3>The checkpoint system</h3>
            <p>
              The Nenek Semukut hiking trail out of Mukut is marked with numbered checkpoints.
              Every approach in this guidebook is described against them.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>CP5</strong> — branch for Batu Naga and Damai Sentosa</li>
              <li>
                <strong>CP7</strong> — branch for Polish Princess, Sam Sam, Naga, Freebird, Puncak Anak
              </li>
              <li><strong>CP9</strong> — far side; the Naga line is visible from here</li>
              <li><strong>CP4–CP5</strong> — base camp with water</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:grid;gap:1.25rem">
          <div class="panel">
            <span class="label">Season</span>
            <h3>When to go</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>March–April</strong> and <strong>August</strong> are the settled windows</li>
              <li>South faces bake after 1&nbsp;pm — start before dawn</li>
              <li>The steepest routes need <strong>two dry days</strong> to come into condition</li>
              <li>
                Monsoon roughly <strong>November–February</strong>: heavy rain, rough seas, ferries
                can stop
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <span class="label">Style &amp; safety</span>
            <h3>What to expect</h3>
            <p>
              Long runouts, some loose rock, mixed bolted and natural protection, jungle pitches to
              the summit, and committing abseil descents frequently finished in the dark.
            </p>
            <p>
              Anchors are being re-equipped over time, but older routes may still be "one bolt plus
              old tat". Helmets. Careful rope management.
            </p>
            <p>
              Not a climber? The <a href="/areas/north-tower/via-ferrata">North Tower via ferrata</a>
              reaches a viewpoint on fixed cable and rungs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
